From: OddBlueDog <3959851+OddBlueDog@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 18 Oct 2026 21:22:42 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] wifi: mt76: mt7915: drop stale MCU replies before waiting for
 one

Commands sent without waiting still get an acknowledgement, which stays
on mcu.res_q until the next waited command discards it by sequence
mismatch. Sequence numbers are only 4 bits wide, so after 15 such
commands an old acknowledgement carries the new command's sequence and
is taken as its response.

The caller holds mcu.mutex and is the only waiter, so anything still
queued when a waited command is sent is stale: purge it first.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 mt7915/mcu.c | 8 ++++++++
 1 file changed, 8 insertions(+)

diff --git a/mt7915/mcu.c b/mt7915/mcu.c
index d8cdd414..6f984e9c 100644
--- a/mt7915/mcu.c
+++ b/mt7915/mcu.c
@@ -240,6 +240,14 @@ mt7915_mcu_send_message(struct mt76_dev *mdev, struct sk_buff *skb,
 
 	mt7915_mcu_set_timeout(mdev, cmd);
 
+	/* We hold mcu.mutex and are about to wait for a reply, so whatever
+	 * is still queued is an ack for a fire-and-forget command or a
+	 * leftover from before a reset. Drop it: with a 4-bit seq, an old
+	 * entry can otherwise be taken as this command's response.
+	 */
+	if (wait_seq)
+		skb_queue_purge(&mdev->mcu.res_q);
+
 	return mt76_tx_queue_skb_raw(dev, mdev->q_mcu[qid], skb, 0);
 }
 
